<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jamaica</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jamaica is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> island that was a British colony from the seventeenth century until 1962, with a plantation economy based on enslaved labour. It adopted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westminster-system">Westminster parliamentary model</a> inherited from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rastafari">Rastafari movement</a>, which emerged there in the 1930s, revered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haile-selassie">Haile Selassie</a>, extending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire</a>'s symbolic influence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jamaica.jpg" alt="Jamaica" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: SKopp, and then manually edited by Zscout370, Madden and others. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Jamaica.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Jamaica covers 10,991 square kilometres and is the third-largest island in the Greater Antilles and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Hispaniola</a>. It lies about 145 km south of Cuba and 191 km west of Hispaniola. Mountains dominate the interior, among them the Blue Mountains in the east, where Blue Mountain Peak rises to 2,256 metres, and a narrow coastal plain surrounds them. The island lies within the main development region for Atlantic tropical cyclones and sometimes suffers heavy storm damage. When the Spanish arrived in 1494 the country was deeply forested, and European settlers later cleared much of it for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> and cultivation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>There is no archaeological evidence of human presence until about 500 CE. A group known as the Redware people, after their pottery, arrived around 600 CE, followed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taino">Taíno</a> around 800 CE, who most likely came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a>. The Taíno lived by farming and fishing, and at their height are thought to have numbered some 60,000 people in around 200 villages headed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cacique">caciques</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Christopher Columbus</a> claimed the island for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> after landing in 1494 on his second voyage. The first Spanish settlement, Sevilla, was founded in 1509 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-de-esquivel">Juan de Esquivel</a> and abandoned around 1524; the capital moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-town">Spanish Town</a>, then St. Jago de la Vega, around 1534.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taino">Taíno</a> died in large numbers from introduced diseases and from enslavement by the Spanish, who then imported enslaved Africans. Many escaped and formed autonomous communities in the remote interior, mixing with the remaining Taíno; these became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaican-maroons">Jamaican Maroons</a>, who kept their freedom for generations under leaders such as Juan de Serras. By the early 17th century an estimated 2,500 to 3,000 people lived on the island.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> conquered Jamaica from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1655. Under British rule it became a leading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> exporter, with a plantation economy that depended on importing enslaved Africans. The British emancipated all slaves in 1838, and many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freedmen">freedmen</a> chose subsistence farms over plantation work. From the 1840s the British brought Chinese and Indian indentured labourers. Independence from the United Kingdom came on 6 August 1962.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Jamaica is a parliamentary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a> and a Commonwealth realm, with a bicameral Parliament, and its constitution dates from 1962. The capital and largest city is Kingston. Most Jamaicans are of African or partly African descent, with minorities of European, Indian, Chinese and Lebanese origin. The Maroon communities of Accompong and elsewhere continue to keep their own traditions. The island is the birthplace of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rastafari">Rastafari</a> religion and of reggae, ska, dub and dancehall music, and its national motto is Out of Many One People.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jamaica” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The empire left an extensive and durable legal inheritance: English common law, procedural traditions and constitutional frameworks were transplanted into dozens of successor states, and the Westminster parliamentary model was adopted, with varying success, across the former empire from India to <b>Jamaica</b> to Nigeria.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-empire">British Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The Rastafari movement, which emerged in <b>Jamaica</b> in the 1930s, gave Haile Selassie messianic status and carried Ethiopian symbolic influence into popular culture around the world.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Overseas commerce grew rapidly under the Navigation Acts, which reserved colonial trade for British ships: sugar from <b>Jamaica</b> and Barbados, tobacco from Virginia and Maryland and textiles from India were re-exported across Europe, and Glasgow prospered on tobacco after the union.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-great-britain">Kingdom of Great Britain · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The United States built the canal between 1904 and 1914, after William Gorgas’s sanitation campaign brought yellow fever and malaria under control, employing tens of thousands of workers, many of them from Barbados, <b>Jamaica</b> and other Caribbean islands, whose descendants form an important Afro-Caribbean, English-speaking community.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-panama">Republic of Panama · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Between 1791 and 1810 more than 25,000 Creoles, planters, petits blancs and free people of colour with their slaves, fled to the United States, <b>Jamaica</b> and Cuba; more than 10,000, expelled from Cuba, reached New Orleans in 1809–1810, nearly doubling its population.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The people now called the Taíno lived across most of the West Indies when Europeans arrived, from the Lucayan Archipelago through Cuba, <b>Jamaica</b>, Hispaniola and Puerto Rico to Guadeloupe.</blockquote><a href="/culture/taino">Taíno · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
